# Summary
Bug introduced: bucket write reservations no longer cover the actual write action, weakening the delete/write synchronization that relied on active_write_reservations.
In this commit, Coordinator::with_bucket_write_handle_for now uses SharedStorageNode::with_bucket_write_snapshot only to load a bucket snapshot/handle and then releases the bucket write reservation before the caller’s write action is executed. Previously, the write action ran while the reservation was held. Because bucket deletion waits only for active_write_reservations to drain, a bucket delete can now race with in-progress writes that no longer hold a reservation. This opens a TOCTOU window where direct writes can complete after a bucket delete (and even after a new bucket with the same name is created by a different owner), causing unauthorized writes or data corruption.

# Fix
Fixed in commit `a2a7f44` (`Restore write reservation lifetime in phase 4`).

The fix keeps the existing storage-owned write reservation primitive but
changes the coordinator helper boundary back to the correct lifetime:

- `Coordinator::with_bucket_write_handle_for(...)` now runs the protected
  action inside `SharedStorageNode::with_bucket_write_snapshot(...)`
- the bucket write reservation therefore covers both bucket-handle loading and
  the full protected write action again
- `DeleteBucket` once again waits for active protected writes to finish before
  deletion can complete

The fix is pinned by the new regression
`delete_bucket_waits_for_bucket_write_handle_action`, which blocks inside the
write-handle action and proves that `DeleteBucket` does not complete until that
blocked action is released.

