# Attack-path analysis
Final: low | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Although the race is real (confirmed by unit test), exploitation requires owner-level credentials and a timing race, and the impact is primarily integrity of the same tenant’s data in a single-tenant deployment. Cross-tenant bucket takeover is not demonstrated in this repo’s default configuration, so the original high severity is not warranted.
## Likelihood
low - Requires authenticated privileges and precise timing to win a race between delete and write; not trivially exploitable at scale.
## Impact
medium - Allows writes to proceed after bucket deletion, risking data integrity and potential writes into a re-created bucket; no confidentiality impact shown and typical deployments are single-tenant.
## Assumptions
- Deployment exposes the HTTP endpoint beyond localhost by configuring ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR or a reverse proxy.
- Attacker has valid S3 credentials with permission to write to and delete the target bucket (or the bucket is configured public-write).
- The vulnerable code paths are exercised by normal S3 write and delete operations (as in the unit test PoC).
- The deployment is primarily single-tenant as described in the threat model; cross-tenant bucket ownership is not demonstrated here.
- Valid S3 credentials with bucket write permission
- Permission to delete the bucket (owner/admin)
- Ability to trigger a concurrent write and delete (race timing)
## Path
[S3 client] -> [with_bucket_write_handle_for] -> [with_bucket_write_snapshot releases reservation]
        \-> [delete_bucket waits active_write_reservations==0] -> [delete completes]
                                              \-> [write action continues]
